<LessonLayout title="4.2 — Prompt Chaining">

  <LessonHeader
    num="4.2"
    title="Prompt Chaining"
    subtitle="Breaking a complex task into a chain of smaller prompts, and validating each step with gate checks."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <LessonToc />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Complicated Tasks Need to Be Broken Down <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Ask a friend who doesn't bake to make a multi-layered decorated cake and just say
      <em>"bake the cake"</em>, and it won't work. You'd give steps: preheat the oven, mix the dry
      ingredients, mix the wet ones, combine, bake layer 1, bake layer 2, make frosting, assemble,
      decorate.
    </p>

    <p>
      LLMs are similar. They're mainly optimized for <strong>single-task input → output</strong>. Give
      them one massive prompt that asks them to juggle dependencies, take actions, and verify results
      all at once, and it's like reciting the whole recipe in one breath: details get missed and the
      result isn't what you expected. They do best when <strong>guided through a process</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>This chapter covers:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Sequential prompting</strong> for multi-step reasoning (below)</li>
      <li><strong>Task chaining:</strong> feeding one step's output into the next step's input</li>
      <li><strong>Gate checks:</strong> programmatic validation between steps</li>
      <li><strong>Implementing gate checks</strong> in code</li>
      <li><strong>Example:</strong> a chain for a multi-part coding assignment (outline → code → validate → refine)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Sequential Prompting <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Sequential prompting</strong> (multi-step reasoning) breaks a complex task into smaller,
      manageable sub-tasks, each handled by <strong>its own prompt</strong>. It's a fundamental building
      block of agentic workflows: instead of one overwhelming command, you guide the model through a
      process.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Example: research → summary → post</h3>

    <p>
      Instead of <em>"Research AI agents, summarize the key concepts, and write a LinkedIn post about
      them"</em>, run three prompts:
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  P1["1️⃣ Research key<br/>concepts of AI agents"]:::step --> R1["📝 concepts"]:::out
  R1 --> P2["2️⃣ Summarize the<br/>previous output"]:::step --> R2["📝 summary"]:::out
  R2 --> P3["3️⃣ Draft a LinkedIn post<br/>from the summary"]:::step --> R3["✅ post"]:::done

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>One big prompt</th><th>Chained prompts</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Model's focus</td><td>Three goals at once</td><td>One goal per step</td></tr>
          <tr><td>When it goes wrong</td><td>Hard to tell which part failed</td><td>You can see which step failed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Improving it</td><td>Rewrite the whole prompt</td><td>Iterate on one step's prompt</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Checking between steps</td><td>Not possible</td><td>Possible (gate checks, coming up)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Try it by hand in a chatbot</h3>

    <p>
      In ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude: paste a long document, ask it to extract what you care about,
      then in the <strong>same chat</strong> ask it to summarize, then to draft the post. The chat
      history carries each answer forward, so you're doing sequential prompting manually.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Why It Works <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>It mirrors how people solve problems:</strong> smaller, more digestible pieces, one goal at a time.</li>
      <li><strong>Better accuracy:</strong> each step is a focused prompt, so its output is more likely to be accurate and relevant.</li>
      <li><strong>Easier to improve:</strong> you can evaluate, iterate, and refine each prompt on its own, using the refinement loop from 3.1.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      This is the same idea as lesson 1.1's "get away with a cheaper model": decompose the task
      yourself, and even a small model can run each step reliably.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Prompt Chaining: Connecting the Steps in Code <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Once each prompt works on its own, the next step is to call the LLM with them
      <strong>in succession, programmatically</strong>. <strong>Prompt chaining</strong> (task chaining)
      connects prompts' inputs and outputs: the output of one LLM call becomes part of the input of the
      next. It runs in your code or in an orchestrator, and it's what turns a manual chat into a
      repeatable, scalable workflow.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  C1["📞 API call 1 · Research<br/>Research key concepts<br/>of AI agents"]:::step --> R1(["RESPONSE_1"]):::out
  R1 -->|"inserted into"| C2["📞 API call 2 · Summarize<br/>Summarize the following<br/>key concepts: RESPONSE_1"]:::step --> R2(["RESPONSE_2"]):::out
  R2 -->|"inserted into"| C3["📞 API call 3 · Draft<br/>Draft a LinkedIn post<br/>based on: RESPONSE_2"]:::step --> F(["FINAL_RESPONSE"]):::done

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef out  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      Unlike the chatbot, an API call remembers nothing (as in 2.1), so your code does the carrying.
      With the notebooks' <code>get_completion</code> helper:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="chain.py" code={chainCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="&quot;Research&quot; without a tool is just recall">
      <p>
        Step 1 says "research", but a plain LLM call can't browse. It can only recall what was in its
        training data, which may be outdated. For real research, step 1 needs a search tool, which
        turns this chain into an agent (4.1).
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Chaining Is Essential for Agents <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Question: <em>"What time is my dental appointment tomorrow?"</em> The answer is in your calendar,
      not in the model. There are two ways to chain the steps that get it.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Option A: hard-coded workflow — your code decides</h3>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  Q["❓ What time is my dental<br/>appointment tomorrow?"]:::q --> L1["🤖 LLM call 1<br/>Does this need<br/>calendar data?"]:::llm
  L1 -->|"Yes"| G["⚙️ Code calls<br/>get_calendar()"]:::code --> L2["🤖 LLM call 2<br/>answer using the<br/>calendar data"]:::llm --> A["✅ Your appointment<br/>is at 9am"]:::done
  L1 -->|"No"| L3["🤖 LLM answers<br/>directly"]:::llm

  classDef q    fill:#f1f5f9,color:#0f172a,stroke:#64748b,stroke-width:2px
  classDef llm  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef code fill:#ffedd5,color:#7c2d12,stroke:#ea580c,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      The first LLM call is used as a <strong>classifier</strong>: a yes/no question whose answer
      your code branches on. The path is fixed in advance; the LLM only fills in each step.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="hard_coded_workflow.py" code={workflowCode} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Option B: AI agent (ReAct) — the LLM decides</h3>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
sequenceDiagram
  participant U as 🙋 User
  participant L as 🤖 LLM
  participant O as ⚙️ Orchestrator
  U->>L: What time is my dental appointment tomorrow?
  L->>O: THOUGHT: I need the calendar.<br/>ACTION: get_calendar("tomorrow")
  O->>O: runs get_calendar() → "9am"
  O->>L: OBSERVATION: "9am"
  L->>O: THOUGHT: It is at 9am.<br/>ACTION: final_answer("9am")
  O->>U: Your appointment is at 9am
    `} />

    <p>
      Same chain of steps (decide → fetch → answer), but no code path was written for this question.
      The LLM chose to call <code>get_calendar</code>; the orchestrator ran it, exactly like the 2.1
      Demand-Spike loop.
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Hard-coded workflow</th><th>AI agent (ReAct)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Who picks the next step</td><td>Your code (if/else on LLM output)</td><td>The LLM, each turn</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Predictability</td><td>High: same path every time</td><td>Lower: path can vary</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Handles new kinds of requests</td><td>Only paths you wrote</td><td>Yes, if it has the right tools</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Testing and debugging</td><td>Easier: test each fixed step</td><td>Harder: many possible paths</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost and latency</td><td>Known number of calls</td><td>Varies with the number of steps</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Either way, it's prompt chaining that links the actions: each step's output feeds the next.
      Rule of thumb: use a fixed workflow when you know the steps, and an agent when the steps depend
      on what's discovered along the way.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Chaining Alone Isn't Reliable <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Each step in a chain can still go wrong. The LLM can <strong>hallucinate</strong>, return the
      <strong>wrong format</strong>, or <strong>not follow an instruction</strong>. And because every
      step trusts its input, one early error derails everything after it: the <strong>domino
      effect</strong>.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  subgraph N["Without checks"]
    direction LR
    A1["Step 1<br/>❌ made-up fact"]:::warn --> A2["Step 2<br/>builds on it"]:::warn --> A3["Step 3<br/>❌ confident,<br/>wrong result"]:::warn
  end
  subgraph Y["With a gate check"]
    direction LR
    B1["Step 1"]:::step --> G{"🚦 Gate check<br/>valid?"}:::gate
    G -->|"pass"| B2["Step 2"]:::step --> B3["Step 3 ✅"]:::done
    G -->|"fail"| R["🔁 retry or stop"]:::warn
  end

  classDef step fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <p>
      You've seen this already: in the 2.1 CoT exercise, the model blamed a promotion that only
      covered Product 2. Had that analysis fed a report-writing step, the report would have repeated
      the error with confidence. Reliable systems need <strong>intermediate validation</strong>
      between steps, which is where gate checks come in.
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Gate Checks: Output Validation <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      A <strong>gate check</strong> is a programmatic validation placed between steps of a chain: a
      quality-control point that an output must pass before it's handed to the next step. Think of
      each step as a brick: the gate check makes sure the brick is solid before you build on it. Gate
      checks rein in the stochastic, unpredictable side of LLMs.
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  S1["🤖 Step N<br/>LLM output"]:::step --> G{"🚦 Gate check<br/>meets criteria?"}:::gate
  G -->|"✅ pass"| S2["🤖 Step N+1"]:::done
  G -->|"❌ fail"| F1["🛑 Raise error, halt"]:::warn
  G -->|"❌ fail"| F2["🔁 Retry the step"]:::retry
  G -->|"❌ fail"| F3["🔁 Retry with the failure<br/>reason in the prompt"]:::retry
  F2 -.-> S1
  F3 -.-> S1

  classDef step  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate  fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn  fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
  classDef retry fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>On failure</th><th>When to use it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Raise an error and halt</td><td>Sensitive data or high-stakes actions, where a wrong output is worse than none</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Retry the step</td><td>Random slips: the model usually gets it right</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Retry with the failure reason</td><td>Systematic mistakes: telling the model <em>what</em> failed makes a repeat less likely</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Three types of gate check</h3>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  G["🚦 Gate checks"]:::gate --> F["📐 Format<br/>is the shape right?"]:::format
  G --> C["📝 Content<br/>is the substance right?"]:::content
  G --> L["🧮 Logic<br/>does it make sense?"]:::logic
  F --> F1["JSON / XML structure<br/>length · required fields<br/>→ Pydantic, structured outputs"]:::detail
  C --> C1["keywords · topics · relevance<br/>quotes exist in source<br/>→ regex, embeddings, LLM judge"]:::detail
  L --> L1["numbers are reasonable<br/>code compiles · no banned imports<br/>→ ast, linters, sanity rules"]:::detail

  classDef gate    fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef format  fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
  classDef content fill:#ccfbf1,color:#134e4a,stroke:#0d9488,stroke-width:2px
  classDef logic   fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef detail  fill:#f8fafc,color:#0f172a,stroke:#94a3b8,stroke-width:1px
    `} />

    <p>
      Checks can be <strong>plain code</strong> (Python) or <strong>LLM-powered</strong>, e.g. a second
      model call that flags harmful or biased text:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Code check</th><th>LLM-powered check</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Good for</td><td>Format, exact matches, syntax, numeric ranges</td><td>Fuzzy judgments: tone, harm, bias, relevance</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Deterministic?</td><td>Yes: same input, same verdict</td><td>No: it's an LLM too, so it can be wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Free, instant</td><td>An extra model call</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Prefer code checks wherever they can do the job; use an LLM check only for what code can't judge.</p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Beyond the course: decision models and determinism (Oct 2026) <SourceBadge source="mine" /></h3>

    <p>
      Two different questions get mixed up when people ask whether an LLM check is "reliable":
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Determinism:</strong> does the same input always give the same verdict?</li>
      <li><strong>Correctness:</strong> is the verdict right? A perfectly deterministic checker can be <em>consistently wrong</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Why LLM checks are non-deterministic (even at temperature 0)</h3>

    <p>
      Sampling (temperature above 0) is the obvious source. The less obvious one: hosted models batch
      your request with other users' requests, and many GPU kernels give slightly different numbers
      depending on batch size. Batch size changes with server load, so a temperature-0 call can still
      return a different answer. Thinking Machines showed in 2025 that <strong>batch-invariant
      kernels</strong> fix this (1,000 identical completions in their test) at some speed cost. Unless
      your provider documents a deterministic mode, assume repeated calls can differ.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Decision models: Jev</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Jev</strong> (TypeSafe AI, early access since Sept 15, 2026) is built for exactly the
      "LLM-powered check" slot. It <strong>doesn't generate text</strong>. You send it state (text or
      JSON) plus typed questions whose answers you define, and it returns one of <em>your</em> answers
      with a probability on every option:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Question type</th><th>Asks</th><th>Returns</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Noul</td><td>Is something true?</td><td>A probability from 0 to 1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Choice</td><td>Which option from a list (up to 255)?</td><td>The pick, a probability per option, a confidence</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Score</td><td>Where on an ordered scale?</td><td>A level, a probability per level, a confidence</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Vendor claims: roughly 70–500 ms per decision and $0.042 per million input tokens (output is
      free), about two orders of magnitude cheaper and faster than an LLM for these narrow judgments.
      These are TypeSafe's own benchmarks.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">So is it deterministic?</h3>

    <p>
      <strong>Not as far as anything published says.</strong> TypeSafe doesn't document a guarantee that
      identical inputs give identical outputs. What changes is the <em>shape</em> of the uncertainty:
    </p>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Code check</th><th>LLM judge</th><th>Decision model (Jev)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Output shape guaranteed?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No: needs its own parse + format gate</td><td>Yes: always one of your options</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same input → same verdict?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Not guaranteed, even at temperature 0</td><td>Not documented</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How uncertainty shows up</td><td>n/a</td><td>A differently worded answer</td><td>A probability you can threshold</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can be confidently wrong?</td><td>Only if the rule is wrong</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes (see below)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cost / latency</td><td>Free, instant</td><td>Highest</td><td>Low (vendor claims)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Good for</td><td>Format, syntax, exact matches</td><td>Judgments that need reasoning</td><td>High-volume narrow judgments: routing, triage, safety flags</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Guaranteed shape doesn't mean correct. TypeSafe itself says it guarantees the shape of answers,
      not that every decision is right. Independent tests found <strong>overconfidence</strong>
      (99.4% stated confidence vs. 90.2% accuracy in the top bucket in one test), and in another,
      swapping option labels dropped accuracy (AUC 0.81 → 0.58): wrong answers hiding inside valid
      structure. It also isn't a calculator, reads dates as text, and can be prompt-injected.
    </p>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Using it as a gate check</h3>

    <p>The pattern people describe as "LLM writes, decision model decides, code acts":</p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  W["🤖 LLM writes<br/>draft / plan / code"]:::llm --> C{"⚙️ Code checks<br/>format · syntax · quotes"}:::code
  C -->|"fail"| R["🔁 retry with reasons"]:::retry
  C -->|"pass"| D["🎯 Decision model<br/>Noul: harmful or biased?<br/>→ probability p"]:::dec
  D --> T{"⚙️ Code acts<br/>on thresholds"}:::code
  T -->|"p < 0.1"| OK["✅ continue"]:::done
  T -->|"p > 0.9"| NO["🛑 block"]:::warn
  T -->|"in between"| H["🧑‍⚖️ escalate to<br/>LLM judge or human"]:::retry

  classDef llm   fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef code  fill:#ffedd5,color:#7c2d12,stroke:#ea580c,stroke-width:2px
  classDef dec   fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef warn  fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
  classDef retry fill:#fef9c3,color:#713f12,stroke:#ca8a04,stroke-width:2px
    `} />

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cheapest checks first:</strong> code, then the decision model, then an LLM judge or a human only for the uncertain band.</li>
      <li><strong>Thresholds are yours to tune:</strong> set them on your own labeled examples, not the vendor's benchmark, because stated probabilities may be overconfident.</li>
      <li><strong>Make runs comparable:</strong> pin the model version and log the probabilities, so a changed verdict can be traced.</li>
    </ul>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Bottom line">
      <p>
        New decision models remove the <em>format</em> problem of LLM-powered checks and make them
        cheap enough to run on every step, but not the <em>correctness</em> problem, and determinism
        isn't guaranteed. Treat them as one fallible layer, not as a code check.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">The pattern in pseudocode</h3>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="gate-check-pseudocode.py" code={gatePseudo} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Example: a research step with format + content checks</h3>

    <p>
      The course's research example checks two things: the output is properly formatted, and every
      quoted passage <strong>actually exists</strong> in the source documents, which catches invented
      quotes. In Python, with Pydantic for the format check:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="research_gate.py" code={researchGate} />

    <LessonCollapsible title="What does findings = [Finding(**item) for item in json.loads(raw)] do?">
      <p>It turns the LLM's raw JSON text into a list of validated <code>Finding</code> objects, and fails loudly if anything is malformed. Read it inside out:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong><code>json.loads(raw)</code></strong> turns the text into Python data: a list of dicts.</li>
        <li><strong><code>for item in …</code></strong> loops over that list; each <code>item</code> is one dict.</li>
        <li><strong><code>Finding(**item)</code></strong> builds one Pydantic object from the dict. <code>**</code> unpacks the dict into keyword arguments: keys become parameter names, values become their values.</li>
        <li><strong><code>[… for …]</code></strong> is a list comprehension: one <code>Finding</code> per dict, collected into a list.</li>
      </ol>

      <CodeBlock lang="python" label="what-it-means.py" code={findingExplained} />

      <p><strong>Why it works as a gate check:</strong> creating a Pydantic object <em>validates</em> it. A mismatch raises an error instead of slipping through:</p>

      <div class="lesson-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>LLM returned</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>An item without <code>source_id</code></td><td><code>ValidationError</code>: field required</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"source_id": "abc"</code></td><td><code>ValidationError</code>: not a valid integer</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>"source_id": "2"</code></td><td>Accepted: Pydantic converts <code>"2"</code> to <code>2</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Sure! Here's the JSON: […]</code></td><td><code>json.loads</code> raises <code>JSONDecodeError</code> before Pydantic runs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A single object instead of a list</td><td>The loop walks the dict's <em>keys</em> (strings), so <code>Finding(**"claim")</code> raises <code>TypeError</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        That's why the line sits in a <code>try</code> that catches all three error types: however the
        format is wrong, you get one readable problem message, which becomes the feedback in the retry
        prompt. If no error is raised, every finding is guaranteed to have a string <code>claim</code>,
        a string <code>quote</code>, and an integer <code>source_id</code>, so the content check below
        can rely on them.
      </p>
    </LessonCollapsible>

    <p>
      Returning a <em>list of problems</em> instead of True/False pays off on retry: the problems are
      exactly what goes into the "retry with the failure reason" prompt.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="retry_with_feedback.py" code={retryLoop} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Use Case: Generating a Data Analysis Script <SourceBadge source="course" /></h2>

    <p>
      Task: a Python script that reads a CSV, calculates the average of a column, and writes the result
      to a new CSV. The whole chain:
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart TB
  P1["1️⃣ Prompt: create an outline"]:::step --> G1{"🚦 Gate 1 · format<br/>a list? mentions read,<br/>process, write?"}:::gate
  G1 -->|"fail"| X1["🛑 Stop or retry"]:::warn
  G1 -->|"pass"| P2["2️⃣ Prompt: write the code<br/>from the outline<br/>csv + statistics only"]:::step
  P2 --> G2{"🚦 Gate 2 · logic<br/>valid Python syntax?"}:::gate
  G2 -->|"pass"| OK["✅ Completed"]:::done
  G2 -->|"fail"| N{"retries left?"}:::gate
  N -->|"yes"| P3["3️⃣ Prompt: refine<br/>code + error messages"]:::retry --> G2
  N -->|"no"| X2["❌ Failed"]:::warn

  classDef step  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate  fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef done  fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
