    `} />

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Step</th><th>Prompt (gist)</th><th>Expected output</th><th>Gate check</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Outline</td><td>"You are a helpful programming assistant… provide a simple step-by-step outline"</td><td>Numbered or bulleted list of high-level steps</td><td>Optional but good: list format, key phrases (read, process, write)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Code</td><td>"Based on the following outline, write the Python code… use standard libraries like csv and statistics. Outline: [step 1 output]"</td><td>Python code</td><td>Syntax check with <code>ast</code> or a linter (flake8, pylint), <strong>without running the code</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Refine (only if gate 2 failed)</td><td>"This code… contains syntax errors: [code]. Error feedback: [gate 2 errors]. Please correct it."</td><td>Corrected code</td><td>Gate 2 again; loop up to a maximum number of tries, then give up</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Step 3 is the "retry with the failure reason" option in action: the error messages from the gate
      become part of the next prompt, which makes this a <strong>feedback loop</strong>.
