    </LessonCollapsible>

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Step 3: the gate check</h3>

    <p>
      Before trusting the code, check its syntax with Python's built-in <code>ast</code> (Abstract
      Syntax Tree) module. <code>ast.parse</code> reads the code without running it:
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="step3_gate.py" code={step3Code} />

    <p>
      The function returns <strong>two</strong> things: a verdict for the code to branch on, and a
      message. If <code>is_valid</code> is <code>False</code>, the optional next step sends the code
      <em>and</em> that message back to the model to fix: the "retry with the failure reason" pattern.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="What this gate can and can't catch">
      <p>
        <strong>False alarm:</strong> models usually wrap code in a Markdown fence
        (<code>```python … ```</code>). Passed straight to <code>ast.parse</code>, perfectly good code
        fails, so strip the fence first.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Missed problems:</strong> the generated script parses fine, but look at what it does
        with bad input. A missing file prints an error and returns quietly, and an empty column writes
        <code>The average is: 0</code> as if 0 were a real result. A syntax gate can't see either; that
        takes a logic gate, e.g. running the script on a small test CSV in a sandbox.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

