    <CodeBlock lang="python" label="script_chain.py" code={scriptChain} />

    <LessonCollapsible title="How the allowed-imports check works">
      <p>
        <code>ast.parse</code> turns the code into a tree of nodes without running it.
        <code>ast.walk(tree)</code> visits every node, and two node types record imports:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>ast.Import</code> for <code>import pandas as pd</code>: each name is in <code>node.names</code>.</li>
        <li><code>ast.ImportFrom</code> for <code>from os import path</code>: the module is in <code>node.module</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <code>.split(".")[0]</code> keeps the top-level package, so <code>os.path</code> counts as
        <code>os</code>. Subtracting the allowed set leaves only the forbidden imports, each reported
        as one problem. Testing it: <code>import pandas</code> and <code>import os.path</code> give
        <code>["Restricted import: os", "Restricted import: pandas"]</code>, while
        <code>import csv, statistics</code> passes.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a cheap <strong>logic check</strong>: it enforces the prompt's "use standard libraries"
        instruction in code instead of trusting the model to follow it, and it's a basic safety net
        before any generated code gets near an interpreter.
      </p>
    </LessonCollapsible>

    <p>The core logic, in three lines:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Break the task into steps.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Use each prompt's output as the next prompt's input.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>Put programmatic gate checks between steps.</strong></li>
    </ol>

